Good afternoon,

Now that the replacement kiosk is in and working, our group's operations dire=
ctor has asked me to
look at putting kiosks in our three sister hotels as well. Before I write up =
a proposal, I wanted
to share how they differ from us, because they aren't all alike.

The harbour hotel. Similar to us: about 120 rooms, a staffed front desk aroun=
d the clock, and a
steady stream of contractors and wedding suppliers. They'd want the same setu=
p we have, with the
badge printer, and the same wording on the screens.

The country house. Much smaller, 28 rooms, and the front desk is closed overn=
ight. Most visitors
are delivery drivers and the odd event planner. They'd probably want the kios=
k only for
contractors, since guests check in with a person.

The city hotel. Mostly business travellers, with meeting rooms that outside g=
roups hire by the
day. The meeting-room visitors are their biggest headache: up to 200 people o=
n a conference day,
all arriving in the same half hour. They're curious whether two kiosks side b=
y side would cope.

Staff. Each hotel has its own front-desk team and its own duty managers. Staf=
f sometimes move
between hotels for a shift, but mostly stay put. Our group IT manager would l=
ike one account for
the whole group if that's possible, but each hotel's manager wants to see onl=
y their own building.

Reporting. The operations director would like a monthly report for each hotel=
, and one for the
group as a whole, so she can compare visitor numbers. If the dashboard can al=
ready do that, that
would be a strong point in the proposal.

Timing. Nothing is decided yet. If the directors agree, the city hotel would =
go first, probably
in the spring, before its busy conference season. The others would follow ove=
r the summer.

What I'll need from you eventually is a rough idea of what each setup would i=
nvolve. I'm not
asking for prices yet; that can come once the directors have read the proposa=
l.

Languages and signage. The city hotel gets a lot of overseas visitors, so the=
 screens would need
at least French, German and Spanish as well as English. The country house wou=
ld like its kiosk
to match the building's old-fashioned look as far as possible; they asked whe=
ther the stand comes
in anything other than black. None of that is a deal-breaker, but it would he=
lp to know what's
possible before I describe each setup to the directors.

Data protection. This is the part our group's data protection lead asked me t=
o check first. Each
hotel is a separate company in law, and she's firm that guest and visitor rec=
ords shouldn't be
visible across them.

So, before anything else: can each hotel's guest sign-ins be kept separate, s=
o one hotel's staff
can't see another's?

